/-!
# From bounded Weil group actions to continuous actions of `ℤ̂`

Deligne, *La conjecture de Weil II*, 1.3.14, shows that an irreducible `ℚ̄_ℓ`-representation of
the Weil group `W` of `X₀` whose restriction to `I = ker deg` is bounded becomes bounded on
all of `W` after a twist (`Representation.exists_isBoundedOn_twist`). The point of the
statement is that a bounded representation of `W` comes from an `ℓ`-adic representation of the
arithmetic fundamental group, i.e. extends continuously to the profinite completion of `W`.
In the proof one uses that the image of `W` then lies in `GL(L)` for an `𝒪_E`-lattice `L`, `E`
a finite extension of `ℚ_ℓ`, and that `GL(L)` is profinite. This file proves the elementary
ingredients of this step, in the basic case `W = ℤ` generated by a Frobenius `F`.

## Linear algebra (eigenvalues of bounded automorphisms)

* `Module.End.norm_eq_one_of_hasEigenvalue`: over any normed field, if the orbits
  `{Fⁿ v | n ∈ ℤ}` of an invertible endomorphism are bounded, every eigenvalue of `F` has norm
  one.
* `Module.End.isWeaklyBounded_range_zpow_apply_iff_norm_eq_one`: over an algebraically closed
  ultrametric normed field (such as `ℚ̄_ℓ = PadicAlgCl ℓ`) and in finite dimension, this is an
  equivalence: the powers of `F` are bounded iff all eigenvalues of `F` are `ℓ`-adic units.
  No diagonalisability is needed: the characteristic polynomial then has integral coefficients
  and `Fⁿ v` is an integral combination of `v, F v, …, F^(d-1) v` (`|n| ≤ 1` for the
  unipotent part, by the ultrametric inequality).
* `Representation.IsBoundedOn.norm_eq_one_of_hasEigenvalue` and
  `Representation.exists_norm_eq_zpow_of_hasEigenvalue`: consequence of Weil II 1.3.14: for an
  irreducible representation of a Weil-type group bounded on `ker deg` there is `b` such that
  all eigenvalues of `ρ(w)` have absolute value `‖b‖ ^ deg w`.

## Continuity of `n ↦ Fⁿ` for the profinite topology

* `LinearEquiv.exists_pow_sub_mem_of_finite_quotient`: pigeonhole in the finite group
  `GL(L ⧸ N)`: if `F` stabilises `N` and `L ⧸ N` is finite, some power `F^m`, `m > 0`, is the
  identity modulo `N`.
* `LinearEquiv.exists_zpow_sub_zpow_mem_ideal_smul_top` and
  `LinearEquiv.exists_zpow_sub_zpow_mem_pow_smul_top`: for a finitely generated `ℤ_ℓ`-module `L`
  (e.g. an `𝒪_E`-lattice) and every `k` there is `m > 0` with `Fⁿ ≡ Fⁿ' (mod ℓᵏ L)` whenever
  `n ≡ n' (mod m)`: `n ↦ Fⁿ` is uniformly continuous from the profinite to the `ℓ`-adic
  topology.
* `LinearEquiv.zhatAction`: for `L ⧸ N` finite, the continuous (locally constant) extension
  `ℤ̂ →* GL(L ⧸ N)` of `n ↦ Fⁿ mod N`, where `ℤ̂` is Mathlib's profinite completion of `ℤ`;
  these are compatible with the reductions `L ⧸ N → L ⧸ N'` (`LinearEquiv.zhatAction_factor`),
  i.e. they form a continuous action of `ℤ̂` on the pro-system `(L ⧸ ℓᵏ L)_k`.

## Extension to `ℤ̂` on a complete lattice

* `LinearEquiv.zhatHom`: if `L` is `I`-adically complete with all `L ⧸ Iᵏ L` finite, the
  finite-level actions glue to a homomorphism `ℤ̂ →* GL(L)` extending `n ↦ Fⁿ`
  (`LinearEquiv.zhatHom_ofInt`), continuous for the `I`-adic topology
  (`LinearEquiv.isLocallyConstant_mkQ_zhatHom_apply`) and unique with these properties
  (`LinearEquiv.eq_of_isLocallyConstant_of_ofInt`).
* `PadicInt.existsUnique_zhat_extension_of_free`: for every automorphism `F` of a finite free
  `ℤ_ℓ`-module `L` (a lattice), `n ↦ Fⁿ` extends uniquely to an `ℓ`-adically continuous
  homomorphism `ℤ̂ →* GL(L)`. Completeness of `L` comes from `IsAdicComplete.pi` and
  `IsAdicComplete.of_linearEquiv`.

To apply this to a bounded `F ∈ GL(V)` over `ℚ̄_ℓ` one still needs that `F` is defined over a
finite extension `E` of `ℚ_ℓ` and stabilises an `𝒪_E`-lattice; over `ℚ̄_ℓ` itself the
valuation ring is not noetherian and `L ⧸ ℓ L` is infinite, so the pigeonhole argument does
not apply directly.
-/

